path: /parent-portal
title: Parent portal for schools: fee balance, receipts and results
description: Parents sign in on their own phone and see their own children only: what is owed, every receipt, attendance day by day, and released results.
nav: Parents
# 4, so it sits with the other things the software DOES (Fees 1, Attendance 2,
# Exams 3) and before what it costs (Pricing 6).
#
# THIS PAGE HAD A nav_order AND NO nav LABEL, which is how it came to be
# invisible: build-site.py puts a page in the nav only if it asks with `nav`,
# so an order on its own is an instruction nobody carries out. It was reachable
# from the footer, which is enough to satisfy the orphan check in
# check-site-seo.py and not nearly enough for a parent to find. A school
# reported exactly that: no mention of the parent portal anywhere on the site.
nav_order: 4
breadcrumb: Parent portal
schema: org software
priority: 0.8
---

<header class="page-hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Parent portal</span>
    <h1>The answer, without the phone call</h1>
    <p class="lede" style="max-width:62ch">
      Most calls to a school office are one of three questions: how much do I
      owe, was my child in school on Tuesday, and what did she get in the test.
      A parent can answer all three themselves, on the phone in their hand, with
      nothing to install.
    </p>
    <div class="hero__cta">
      <a class="btn btn--primary btn--lg" href="/pricing" data-app="signup">Start 14 day free trial</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost btn--lg" href="/contact" data-app="portal">Open the portal</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- ============================================================ FOR PARENTS =
     WHY THIS SITS SECOND, ABOVE EVERYTHING ELSE ON THE PAGE.

     Every other word on this page is addressed to a school that is deciding
     whether to buy: "Start 14 day free trial", "More questions principals
     ask", "not something a school should have to police". That is correct for
     a feature page and useless to the person the header's Parent sign in
     button sends here with no script, and to any parent who searches for the
     portal and lands on it.

     They need two things and neither was anywhere on this site: the way in,
     and where an account comes from when they have not been given one. The
     second is the one that generated the report. A parent cannot create their
     own account, by design, so "sign up" is not an answer and neither is
     silence. The answer is "ask your school", said out loud.
-->
<section class="section" id="for-parents">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="card" style="max-width:800px; margin: 0 auto; text-align: center; padding: 48px 32px; border-top: 4px solid var(--brand);">
      <span class="eyebrow" style="justify-content: center;">For parents</span>
      <h2 style="margin-top:12px">Are you a parent?</h2>
      <p style="margin-top:16px; font-size: 16.5px; color: var(--ink-2);">
        Sign in with the email address and password your child&rsquo;s school
        gave you. You will see your own children, and nobody else&rsquo;s.
      </p>
      <div style="margin-top:24px; display: flex; justify-content: center;">
        <a class="btn btn--primary btn--lg" href="/contact" data-app="portal">Parent sign in</a>
      </div>
      <div class="note" style="margin-top:32px; text-align: left;">
        <p>
          <b>No account yet?</b> Only your child&rsquo;s school can create one.
          Contact the school office and ask them to make you a parent account:
          they will give you the email address and password to use. There is
          nothing here for you to buy and no form to fill in, and it takes the
          school a few seconds.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top:12px">
          <b>Forgotten your password?</b> Ask the office as well. The address a
          school gives you does not have to be a real mailbox, so a reset email
          may never arrive, and the office can look your password up and read
          it back to you.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>What a parent sees, and what they cannot</h2>
    <div class="grid grid--2" style="margin-top:32px">
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card__icon">
          <svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M17 20h5v-2a3 3 0 00-5.356-1.857M17 20H7m10 0v-2c0-.656-.126-1.283-.356-1.857M7 20H2v-2a3 3 0 015.356-1.857M7 20v-2c0-.656.126-1.283.356-1.857m0 0a5.002 5.002 0 019.288 0M15 7a3 3 0 11-6 0 3 3 0 016 0zm6 3a2 2 0 11-4 0 2 2 0 014 0zM7 10a2 2 0 11-4 0 2 2 0 014 0z"></path></svg>
        </div>
        <h3>Their own children, all of them</h3>
        <p>
          One login for the family. A father with three children in the school
          sees all three, with one combined balance and the per child breakdown
          underneath, which is the same shape as the receipt he was handed.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card__icon" style="background: var(--money-50); color: var(--money-700);">
          <svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 12h6m-6 4h6m2 5H7a2 2 0 01-2-2V5a2 2 0 012-2h5.586a1 1 0 01.707.293l5.414 5.414a1 1 0 01.293.707V19a2 2 0 01-2 2z"></path></svg>
        </div>
        <h3>Every receipt, not just the balance</h3>
        <p>
          The full history: what was charged, what was paid, when, and by which
          receipt number. A parent who thinks a payment is missing can check
          before they ring, and usually finds it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card__icon" style="background: var(--due-50); color: var(--due-800);">
          <svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M8 7V3m8 4V3m-9 8h10M5 21h14a2 2 0 002-2V7a2 2 0 00-2-2H5a2 2 0 00-2 2v12a2 2 0 002 2z"></path></svg>
        </div>
        <h3>Attendance day by day</h3>
        <p>
          Present, absent, late, half day or leave, per day. Not a percentage
          with no detail behind it, because a percentage is exactly what
          starts the argument a school wants to avoid.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card__icon card__icon--accent">
          <svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 19v-6a2 2 0 00-2-2H5a2 2 0 00-2 2v6a2 2 0 002 2h2a2 2 0 002-2zm0 0V9a2 2 0 012-2h2a2 2 0 012 2v10m-6 0a2 2 0 002 2h2a2 2 0 002-2m0 0V5a2 2 0 012-2h2a2 2 0 012 2v14a2 2 0 01-2 2h-2a2 2 0 01-2-2z"></path></svg>
        </div>
        <h3>Results, once you release them</h3>
        <p>
          Nothing appears until the school publishes it. Marks being entered and
          results being visible are two separate events, so a teacher can work
          on Tuesday and the school can announce on Friday.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Two things it deliberately does not do</h2>
    <ul class="checklist" style="margin-top:22px">
      <li>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 10.5l4 4 8-9" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
        <span><b>Parents cannot pay through it.</b> Cash, bank transfer and wallet payments are all recorded by the office, but there is no online payment for school fees. Saying so costs a few visitors and saves every one of them a surprise.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 10.5l4 4 8-9" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
        <span><b>Parents cannot open their own account.</b> The school creates the login and hands it over, which is deliberate: an open registration form on a page listing children is not something a school should have to police.</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:20px">
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/faq">More questions principals ask</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

